<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kuttamuwa stele</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 735 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kuttamuwa stele is a basalt funerary monument found in 2008 at Zincirli in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, ancient Sam'al. Set up around 735 BCE by Kuttamuwa, an official of King Panamuwa II, it shows him at a funerary feast, and its inscription asks that offerings be made for his soul, which dwells in the stone. It is important evidence for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> beliefs about the soul.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kuttamuwa-stele.jpg" alt="Kuttamuwa stele" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dosseman · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gaziantep_Archaeology_museum_Kuttamuwa_stele_4270.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
